{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>But the prophet who presumes, etc.</b> One must know: (a) why it says <b>אַךְ</b> (but), and also why it says <b>אֲשֶׁר יָזִיד</b> (who presumes) and was not satisfied to say ״who speaks״ etc.; (b) why it says <b>וְכִי תֹאמַר בִּלְבָבְךָ</b> (and if you say in your heart) etc. — <b>אֲשֶׁר יְדַבֵּר</b> (who speaks) etc. — <b>וְלֹא יִהְיֶה הַדָּבָר</b> (and the thing does not come) etc. — it is obvious that if what he commanded does not come about, then HaShem did not speak that thing; (c) why it says <b>אֲשֶׁר יְדַבֵּר בְּשֵׁם ה׳</b> (who speaks in the name of YHWH) — why in the preceding verse did it say ״בִּשְׁמִי״ (in My name) while in this verse it says ״בְּשֵׁם ה׳״ (in the name of YHWH); (d) why it says <b>הוּא הַדָּבָר</b> (that is the thing) — he should have said only ״לֹא דִבְּרוֹ ה׳״ (HaShem did not speak it), and I know that it refers back to that thing." "<b>Now,</b> the matter will be explained in light of what the prophet said in the episode of Jehoshaphat and Ahab in the battle of Ramoth Gilead (1 Kings 22), where all the prophets spoke with one accord to Ahab, ״עֲלֵה וְהַצְלַח״ (Go up and succeed), and from the words of Micaiah son of Imlah it was revealed that a spirit had gone out from before HaShem to entice Ahab to go up and fall in battle. And in my ḥiddushim on the words of the Prophets I raised the difficulty: why at first did the spirit of Naboth pass upon Micaiah and he prophesied like the first prophets and said ״עֲלֵה וְהַצְלַח״, and only afterward, in a later prophecy, did he speak the prophecy of HaShem, and the spirit of Naboth was of no avail before it? And regarding the spirit too I wonder: how did it not fulfill its mission that it went out to accomplish? It is further difficult concerning Micaiah: why did he nullify the counsel of HaShem, heaven forfend, and His thoughts — which He devised to bring Ahab down in the battle of Ramoth Gilead — by coming and revealing His secret to Ahab himself?" "<b>Now,</b> these are the matters that HaShem aroused through the pleasantness of His words: since all is revealed and foreseen before the Creator, for a time will come when a spirit will go out from before Him and mislead the prophets, and even though they spoke falsely, they are nonetheless coerced. Therefore, when He came to command the punishment of the prophet, He prefaced and said <b>אַךְ הַנָּבִיא</b> — אַךְ (but) limits it to a lesser prophet, in a similar case, who speaks a thing and it does not come about and he does not die. And He was precise in saying <b>אֲשֶׁר יָזִיד</b> (who presumes) to alert you that only a prophet who presumes to speak etc. shall die — meaning, but a prophet who spoke falsely and did not presume, such as one whom a misleading spirit caused to err — for this one bears no guilt, since the spirit went out with HaShem's consent, and those who prophesy from its side are innocent of the transgression. And although in Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 89a) they said that Zedekiah son of Chenaanah was liable even though the spirit of Naboth misled him, that is for the reason they stated there, namely that he should have reasoned that two prophets do not prophesy in the same style; but aside from this he would have been exempt. And although four hundred prophets said the same thing, and Micaiah son of Imlah also said ״עֲלֵה וְהַצְלַח״ and concluded — despite all this, all of them opened their mouths to prophesy and the spirit spoke through their mouths, and when they sensed that they were prophesying in one style they did not continue to speak further on that matter — unlike Zedekiah. And concerning this it says ״אֲשֶׁר יָזִיד וְגוֹ׳ וּמֵת״ (who presumes etc. and shall die) etc." "<b>And now,</b> here the question arises: by what shall we know the thing etc.? And if from the fact that the thing does not come about — we are concerned about a misleading spirit of Naboth and the like, so why should the prophet be put to death, since the spirit that went out by the word of HaShem misled him? And similarly in the matter: if in the episode of Ramoth Micaiah had not come and revealed the secret, all four hundred prophets would have been liable to death though they committed no violence — and HaShem loves justice. And that is what it says: <b>וְכִי תֹאמַר</b> (and if you say) etc. — <b>אֵיכָה נֵדַע הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר לֹא דִבְּרוֹ ה׳</b> (how shall we know the thing that HaShem did not speak) — here it included the word of HaShem to the prophet and also included the word of HaShem to the spirit: ״צֵא וַעֲשֵׂה כֵן״ (Go out and do so)." "<b>And it says: who speaks the prophet</b> etc. — meaning, if the prophet utters his prophecy in the name of YHWH — and this is what He was precise in saying: <b>בְּשֵׁם ה׳ וְלֹא יִהְיֶה הַדָּבָר</b> (in the name of YHWH and the thing does not come) etc. — then you shall know that the prophet spoke it with presumption, meaning, it did not come from the side of a spirit that misled him. The reason is that a false spirit will not rest upon the honored name of YHWH, for HaShem is Elohim of truth. And that is what it says: <b>הוּא הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר לֹא דִבְּרוֹ ה׳</b> (that is the thing that HaShem did not speak) — meaning: this is what I tell you concerning it, that HaShem did not speak it, and it did not come through a spirit either but through presumption, and that is what it says: <b>בְּזָדוֹן דִבְּרוֹ</b> (in presumption the prophet spoke it). But there is another matter, which although it is false the Torah cannot tell you ״HaShem did not speak it; in presumption the prophet spoke it״ — and that is when the prophet does not speak in the name of YHWH, for even though it be false, one must suspect a spirit, like the spirit of Naboth." "<b>And with this</b> the matter of Micaiah becomes agreeable to a person's soul, for at first he did not mention the name of HaShem, and therefore he was speaking nothing but words of the [deceiving] spirit, until Jehoshaphat became wise and said to him (II Chronicles 18:15): ״אֲנִי מַשְׁבִּיעֲךָ אֲשֶׁר לֹא תְדַבֵּר אֵלַי רַק אֱמֶת בְּשֵׁם ה׳״ — meaning, he became wise to state to him the condition that HaShem stipulated here, saying אֲשֶׁר יְדַבֵּר הַנָּבִיא בְּשֵׁם ה׳ — according to what we explained, that he must speak בְּשֵׁם ה׳, and by this, if there is a misleading spirit, it will clear the way. And so it was: when he mentioned to him the name of HaShem and said הַנָּבִיא בְּשֵׁם ה׳, the [deceiving] spirit fled from him, and the prophet spoke the truth as it truly is. And with this all the fine points we examined regarding the prophecy of Ramoth Gilead are resolved; and even though we do not find that Micaiah mentioned the name of HaShem, he did mention it, and the verse is elliptical — since Jehoshaphat mentioned it and Micaiah directed himself toward the Name that Jehoshaphat had mentioned, it was effective. And do not find it difficult from his statement there appearing several times ״אֲנִי מַשְׁבִּיעֲךָ וְגוֹ׳ בְּשֵׁם ה׳״ — which would seem to indicate that he adjured him several times to speak בְּשֵׁם ה׳ — for perhaps Micaiah was not sensitive to this fine point of Jehoshaphat except on the last occasion:" "<b>Or,</b> it is possible that the phrase ״בְּשֵׁם ה׳״ does not attach to the phrase ״אֲנִי מַשְׁבִּיעֲךָ״, but is an independent statement that he introduced on the last occasion. And according to this it is not difficult why Micaiah revealed the sod of HaShem — for let the world be destroyed rather than the source of truth be altered to unify His name upon a falsehood, heaven forfend — and all the more so since in any event the [deceiving] spirit accomplished its effect, for Ahab did not believe him and went up and fell, and thus the reckoning of HaShem was fulfilled, and the falsehood is not attributed to the name of HaShem, the Elohim of truth."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}],